Why do we need Saga?

Suppose an online banking application has three microservices:

Account Service
Payment Service
Notification Service
Scenario

A customer transfers ₹10,000.

Account Service
      ↓
Debit ₹10,000

      ↓
Payment Service
Record Transaction

      ↓
Notification Service
Send SMS

If the Payment Service fails after the amount is debited, the system becomes inconsistent unless the debit is reversed.

The Saga Pattern solves this by executing a compensating transaction.

Real-Time Banking Example

Customer transfers ₹10,000.

Step 1

Account Service

Balance = ₹50,000

↓

Debit ₹10,000

↓

Balance = ₹40,000
Step 2

Payment Service

Create transaction record

Suppose the database is unavailable.

Payment Failed
Step 3 (Compensation)

Account Service

Credit ₹10,000 back

↓

Balance = ₹50,000

The customer never loses money even though the overall operation failed.

